OSP User Authentication and Authorization
Open Service Platform

!!! Introduction

The Open Service Platform provides some basic support for user authentication and authorization,
based on the Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthService interface. The OSP core library itself does not provide
an implementation of the <[AuthService]> interface. A very simple implementation of the interface
is provided by the <*SimpleAuthService*> sample, found in the <[samples]> directory.

Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthService defines the methods <[authenticate()]>, <[authenticateAccount()]>
and <[authorize()]>. The first two are used to authenticate a user based on a user name and
credentials/password combination. The last one is used to verify that a given user has a certain
permission.

!!! Authentication

Authentication in OSP is based on user name and credentials. In the simplest case, the credentials
used to authenticate a user consist of a simple password. However, more complex schemes (e.g., based
on certificates) are theoretically possible. An implementation of the <[AuthService]> normally
is based on a database of user names and passwords. The <[authenticate()]> member function of
a subclass of <[Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthService]> has to verify that the given combination of
user name and credentials is a valid one. The <[authenticate()]> member function is defined
as follows:

    bool authenticate(const std::string& userName, const std::string& credentials) const;
----

A user may be able to log in with several names, for example with the account name or the user
principal name of a directory service. The <[authenticateAccount()]> member function checks the
credentials like <[authenticate()]> and also returns the account name, under which the service
knows the user:

    bool authenticateAccount(const std::string& userName, const std::string& credentials,
        std::string& accountName) const;
----

The other members of the service, such as <[authorize()]>, take the account name. So an
application logs users in with <[authenticateAccount()]> and keeps the returned account name,
for example in the session, for the later calls of the service. A web application gives the
session a new ID at the login with Poco::OSP::Web::WebSessionManager::renew(), so that a session
ID known before the login cannot be used for the session of the logged-in user (see the
documentation of the Web bundle):

    std::string accountName;
    if (pAuthService->authenticateAccount(userName, password, accountName))
    {
        pSession = pSessionManager->renew(appName, request, pSession);
        pSession->setValue<std::string>("username", accountName);
    }
----

The default implementation of <[authenticateAccount()]> calls <[authenticate()]> and returns
the given user name as the account name.

!!! Authorization

Authorization in OSP is based on the concept of permissions. In OSP, a permission is a simple string
denoting a certain privilege. The <[authorize()]> method of Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthService checks
whether a user identified by a user name has a certain permission. How user names and permissions
are associated is up to the actual implementation of <[AuthService]>. The possibilities range from
a simple configuration file specifying the permissions for each user name to a sophisticated
authorization concept based on user groups and roles.
For the most part, OSP does not care about user permissions. For example, the Web bundle allows
to specify a required permission for accessing a certain path on the server. How the permission
is named is not of interest to the Web library -- it simply checks that the user (obtained from
HTTP Basic authentication) has the specified permission.

The <[authorize()]> member function is defined as follows:

    bool authorize(const std::string& userName, const std::string& permission) const;
----


!!! The SimpleAuthService Sample

A very simple implementation of <[AuthService]> can be found in the
<[samples/SimpleAuthService]> directory. The sample is for testing on
this computer only. The <[com.appinf.osp.simple-auth]> bundle provides
the same service with hashed passwords in the configuration.

The <[SimpleAuthService]> only knows two users -- "admin" and "user".
Their passwords are set (as clear text) in the global configuration file
with the "adminPassword" and "userPassword" properties, e.g.:

	adminPassword = s3cr3t
	userPassword = t0p53cr3t
----

There is no default password. Without "adminPassword", the service is
not registered and the bundle logs an error. Without "userPassword",
"user" cannot log in. The service compares the SHA-256 digests of the
passwords in constant time.

The "admin" user has all permissions in the checks without a role or
scope. The service knows no roles or scopes, so it denies every check that
is restricted to one (<[authorize()]> with a role or scope), also for
"admin". By default, "user" has no
permissions. The set of permissions for "user" can be set in the global
configuration file, using the "userPermissions" property. The permissions
are specified as a comma-separated list, e.g.

    userPermissions = somePermission, someOtherPermission
----

The service is registered under the service name "osp.auth" and
can be obtained using the Poco::OSP::ServiceFinder helper class:

    Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthService::Ptr pAuthService =
        Poco::OSP::ServiceFinder::findByName<Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthService>(pContext, "osp.auth");
----


!!! Database and LDAP-based Authentication - The AuthAdminService

OSP comes with an extended implementation of the <[AuthService]>. The service
is called <[AuthAdminService]> and is defined and
implemented in the <[com.appinf.osp.auth]> bundle. As its name implies the service
not only provides the <[authenticate()]> and <[authorize()]> methods known from
the <[Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthService]>, it also provides methods for managing
user accounts and permissions. There is also a shell command available for managing
users, roles and permissions.

The following features are supported:
  * User and role-based permission grants.
  * User accounts and permissions are stored in a SQL Database (other database
    types can be supported as well).
  * LDAP server integration. Users can be authenticated via an LDAP server and
    permissions and roles can be assigned via LDAP attributes.
  * In-memory caching of password hashes and permissions.

User authentication and authorization is based on the concepts of users, roles and
permissions. Every user account can be assigned a list of permissions and a list of roles.
Every role can be assigned a list of permissions as well.
The effective permissions of a user are the permissions directly granted to him,
as well as the permissions granted via its assigned roles.


!! Setting up the Authentication/Authorization Database

The default <[AuthAdminService]> implementation in the <[com.appinf.osp.auth.data]>
bundle uses a SQL database for managing users, roles and
permissions. In order to use the service, a database must be set up and the
service must be configured to use a specific database. The Data library from
the POCO C++ Libraries is used for database access, therefore the service
is independent from the underlying database engine. ODBC, MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQLite
can be used.

Following is the database schema required by the <[AuthAdminService]>, as created
by <[Auth/sql/createdb.sql]>. A password hash of version 4 (see <*auth.hashVersion*>)
has more than 64 characters:

    CREATE TABLE auth_user
    (
        username VARCHAR(256) PRIMARY KEY,
        password VARCHAR(256) NOT NULL,
        auth_failures INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0
    );

    CREATE TABLE auth_role
    (
        rolename VARCHAR(256) PRIMARY KEY
    );

    CREATE TABLE auth_user_roles
    (
        username VARCHAR(256) NOT NULL REFERENCES auth_user(username) ON DELETE CASCADE,
        rolename VARCHAR(256) NOT NULL REFERENCES auth_role(rolename) ON DELETE CASCADE
    );

    CREATE TABLE auth_user_permissions
    (
        username VARCHAR(256) NOT NULL REFERENCES auth_user(username) ON DELETE CASCADE,
        permission VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL
    );

    CREATE TABLE auth_role_permissions
    (
        rolename VARCHAR(256) NOT NULL REFERENCES auth_role(rolename) ON DELETE CASCADE,
        permission VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL
    );

    CREATE TABLE auth_user_attributes
    (
        username VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL REFERENCES auth_user(username) ON DELETE CASCADE,
        attribute VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,
        value VARCHAR(256) NOT NULL
    );

    CREATE TABLE auth_role_attributes
    (
        rolename VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL REFERENCES auth_role(rolename) ON DELETE CASCADE,
        attribute VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,
        value VARCHAR(256) NOT NULL
    );

    CREATE TABLE auth_role_mappings
    (
        external_role VARCHAR(256) PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES auth_role(rolename) ON DELETE CASCADE,
        internal_role VARCHAR(256) NOT NULL REFERENCES auth_role(rolename) ON DELETE CASCADE
    );
----

The column <[auth_failures]> counts the failed logins of a user. Without it,
users are not locked out after <*auth.maxFailures*> failed logins, and a
warning is logged at startup.

After setting up the tables, at least one initial user account must be created.
Usually, this will be an administrator account with the necessary permissions to
perform further administrative tasks using the OSP Shell service.

The following statement creates a user named "admin" with password "admin":

    INSERT INTO auth_user (username, password) VALUES('admin', '3d29e163b9107fcb31077b45b47026e2');
----

If LDAP authentication is enabled, the following statement marks "admin" as a
local user, so that its password is checked against the database and not by
the LDAP server:

    INSERT INTO auth_user_attributes VALUES('admin', 'auth_ldap', 'false');
----

The password is not stored in clear text in the database. Only a salted hash of the
password is stored. The salted hash of the password is computed by concatenating
the salt with the password and computing the MD5:

    passwordHash = MD5(salt | password)
----

The salt can be configured in the bundle.properties file in the <[com.appinf.osp.auth.data]>
bundle (property <[auth.passwordSalt]>) and should be unique for each application.

On Linux systems, the has can be computed with the md5sum command:

    $ echo -n "saltpassword" | md5sum
----

On OS X and BSD systems, the md5 utility can be used:

    $ md5 -s "saltpassword"
----

<!Important!>: MD5 hashes are no longer considered secure. The AuthAdminService
replaces the initial MD5 password hash with a hash of the configured version
(PBKDF2, see <*auth.hashVersion*>) upon the first successful login.
Nevertheless, only the initial account should be created this way.

The following permissions used by OSP should be granted to the administrator:

  * bundleAdmin: allow access to the <*BundleAdmin*> web application.
  * authAdmin: allow access to the <[auth]> Shell command.

This can be done with the following SQL statements:

    INSERT INTO auth_user_permissions VALUES('admin', 'bundleAdmin');
    INSERT INTO auth_user_permissions VALUES('admin', 'authAdmin');
----

Instead of granting the permissions directly to the user account, a role
can be used instead. In this case, the permissions are granted to the role, and
then the role is assigned to one or more user accounts:

    INSERT INTO auth_role VALUES('systemAdmin');
    INSERT INTO auth_role_permissions VALUES('systemAdmin', 'bundleAdmin');
    INSERT INTO auth_role_permissions VALUES('systemAdmin', 'authAdmin');
    INSERT INTO auth_user_roles VALUES('admin', 'systemAdmin');
----

After the database tables have been set up, the service must be configured.


!! Configuring the AuthAdminService

The <[AuthAdminService]> default implementation in the <[com.appinf.osp.auth.data]> bundle
is configured using the global application configuration file. At least the
<*auth.db.connector*> and <*auth.db.connectionString*> configuration
properties must be specified.

The following configuration properties are available:

  * auth.db.connector
  * auth.db.connectionString
  * auth.db.sessionTimeout
  * auth.db.databaseStartupTimeout
  * auth.poolSize
  * auth.cacheSize
  * auth.cacheTimeout
  * auth.hashVersion
  * auth.saltLength
  * auth.pbkdf2Rounds
  * auth.credentialsSalt
  * auth.maxFailures
  * auth.lockOutTime
  * auth.neverLockOutUsers
  * auth.ldap.uri
  * auth.ldap.authMode
  * auth.ldap.bindDN
  * auth.ldap.bindUser
  * auth.ldap.bindPassword
  * auth.ldap.bindMode
  * auth.ldap.searchBase
  * auth.ldap.userObjectClass
  * auth.ldap.groupObjectClass
  * auth.ldap.timeout
  * auth.ldap.permissionAttribute
  * auth.ldap.roleAttribute
  * auth.ldap.rolePermissionAttribute
  * auth.ldap.roleFilter
  * auth.ldap.localRoles
  * auth.ldap.localUsers
  * auth.ldap.extraAttributes
  * auth.ldap.inaccessible.fallbackToLocal
  * auth.ldap.cache.lifetimeDays


! auth.db.connector

This setting specifies which SQL database backend to use for accessing the database.
Valid values are "ODBC", "MySQL", "PostgreSQL" and "SQLite".
The respective POCO Data bundle (poco.data.odbc, poco.data.mysql, poco.data.postgresql or
poco.data.sqlite) must be available as well.

If this setting is not specified, the <[AuthAdminService]> will be disabled.


! auth.db.connectionString

This setting specifies the connection string for connecting to the specific database
instance. For SQLite, this is simply the path of the database file to use.
For MySQL, the format of this string is:

    user=<username>;password=<password>;db=<database>;compress=true|false;auto-reconnect=true|false
----

For ODBC, please refer to your system's ODBC documentation for obtaining the connection string.


! auth.db.sessionTimeout

The time in seconds after which a database session that has not been used is
discarded and a new one is created. This is not the connection timeout of the
connection string. Default is 0, which disables the timeout.


! auth.db.databaseStartupTimeout

The time in seconds for which the bundle retries to connect to the database at
its start, every two seconds. Default is 10. If the database cannot be used after
that time, an error is logged and the service is not registered.


! auth.poolSize

The number of database sessions that serve the requests to the service at the same
time. Default is 1. With a value greater than 1, the service is a
Poco::OSP::Auth::PooledAuthAdminService with that number of <[AuthAdminService]>
instances, each with a session of its own.


! auth.cacheSize

This setting allows configuring the maximum number of entries in the
internal permissions cache. This is used to reduce database accesses for
user authorization and thus speed up operation. Default is 1024. Should
be at least the number of maximum concurrent users expected on the
system.


! auth.cacheTimeout

This setting allows configuring the timeout of the password cache. The
password cache is used to reduce database accesses or LDAP lookups (if
LDAP authentication is enabled).


! auth.hashVersion

Specifies the version of the password hashing algorithm.

  - Version 1 uses MD5 with a global salt. This is considered insecure and should
    no longer be used.
  - Version 2 uses PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA1 and per-user random salt.
  - Version 3 uses PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA1 and per-user random salt with additional
    MD5 password hashing allowing implementation of secure challenge-response
    authentication mechanisms such as SCRAM-SHA1.
  - Version 4 uses PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256 and per-user random salt with additional
    SHA-1 password hashing, with at least 50000 iterations and a salt of at least
    8 bytes (default, recommended).

The default version is 4. A stored hash of a lower version than the configured one,
and a stored hash of the configured version (3 or 4) with fewer iterations than configured,
is replaced when the user successfully authenticates. A login that the LDAP server
has accepted and that stores the user's LDAP data also replaces a stored hash that
the password does not match. A login that checks the password of an unknown user,
or of a user without a stored hash, takes about as long as the check of a stored hash
of the configured version and iteration count.

A value other than 1 to 4 fails the start of the <[com.appinf.osp.auth.data]> bundle.


! auth.saltLength

The length of the generated per-user salt in bytes. Defaults to 7. Version 4 uses
at least 8 bytes.


! auth.pbkdf2Rounds

The number of iterations for the PBKDF2 algorithm. Defaults to 1024; version 4
uses at least 50000 iterations, whatever this setting is.
Note that existing Version 2 hashes cannot be validated anymore if this value is
changed. Version 3 and 4 hashes store their number of iterations and are not affected.
A value of 0 or less fails the start of the <[com.appinf.osp.auth.data]> bundle.


! auth.credentialsSalt

An additional salt value for the initial MD5 (Version 3) or SHA-1 (Version 4) hash
that is the input for the main PBKDF2 hash. Defaults to "poco". Can be left at default
value. If changed while in deployment, Version 3 and 4 hashes cannot be validated anymore.


! auth.maxFailures

The number of failed logins after which a user is locked out for
<*auth.lockOutTime*> seconds. Default is 0, which disables the lock-out. The failed
logins are counted in the column <[auth_failures]> of the user table.

A login of a user that is not an LDAP user counts as a failed login if the
password does not match the stored hash or the user has no stored hash. Which
logins of an LDAP user count is described in the section on LDAP below. A login
refused during a lock-out, or one that fails because the account or its stored
password hash changed during the login, is not counted.

The count is kept until a login of the user accepts the password or the user is
unlocked, so after a lock-out has ended, the next failed login locks the user out
again. While a user is locked out, every login of the user is refused, and the web
server does not authenticate the user's sessions and bearer tokens. The
<*auth unlock*> shell command (Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthAdminService::unlockOutUser())
ends a lock-out and resets the count.


! auth.lockOutTime

The time in seconds for which a user is locked out after <*auth.maxFailures*>
failed logins. Default is 600. A value of 0 disables the lock-out.


! auth.neverLockOutUsers

A comma- or semicolon-separated list of user names that failed logins never lock
out, for example the main administrator account, so that failed logins of others
with its name cannot lock the administrator out. A name is compared exactly with
the account name of the login, whose ASCII letters are in lower case for an LDAP
user. The lock-out of the second factor (see Two-Factor Authentication below)
applies to these users too. A login of a local user under a name that the
database collation treats as the same user name (another letter case, other
accents, trailing spaces) is not exempted and can still lock the user out.


! auth.ldap.uri

If user authentication via LDAP is enabled, this setting specifies the
URI of the LDAP server to use. Example: <[ldap://ldap-server.company.com]>
(unencrypted connection) or <[ldaps://ldap-server.company.com]> (secure
connection).

Only an <[ldaps://]> URI keeps passwords off the network in clear text:
with an <[ldap://]> URI, the password of every user who logs in, and the
password of <*auth.ldap.bindUser*>, are sent to the LDAP server
unencrypted. StartTLS is not supported.

The LDAP settings are checked at startup. An error is logged for a setting
that cannot be used: an <*auth.ldap.authMode*>, or in the ACCOUNT mode an
<*auth.ldap.bindMode*>, that is not one of its values, a DN template of the
DN mode without exactly one placeholder (see <*auth.ldap.bindDN*>), or an
<*auth.ldap.roleFilter*> or <*auth.ldap.localRoles*> that is not a valid
regular expression. The LDAP server is then not used: a login of an LDAP
user is handled as when the LDAP directory cannot be used (see the section
on LDAP below). If OSP Auth is built without LDAP support, an error is logged
when <*auth.ldap.uri*> is set, and every user is checked against the
database.


! auth.ldap.authMode

The way the entry of a user is found: DN (the default) or ACCOUNT, in any
letter case. In the DN mode, the user binds with the DN built from
<*auth.ldap.bindDN*> (or <*auth.ldap.searchBase*>). In the ACCOUNT mode, the
entry of the user is searched below <*auth.ldap.searchBase*> by its
<[sAMAccountName]> or <[userPrincipalName]> (see <*auth.ldap.bindMode*> and the
section on LDAP below).


! auth.ldap.bindDN

If user authentication via LDAP is enabled, in the DN mode, this setting
specifies the distinguished name (DN) to bind to in order to authenticate a
user. The DN must contain the placeholder %s exactly once, which will be
replaced during authentication with the actual username. Example:

    uid=%s,cn=users,dc=orgunit,dc=company,dc=com
----

The user name is escaped as an LDAP DN attribute value before it replaces
the placeholder. If <*auth.ldap.bindDN*> is not set, the DN is built from
<*auth.ldap.searchBase*> instead, and a warning is logged at startup.


! auth.ldap.bindUser

The name with which the service account binds in the SERVICE bind mode of
the ACCOUNT mode (see <*auth.ldap.bindMode*>). Not used in the DN mode.


! auth.ldap.bindPassword

The password of <*auth.ldap.bindUser*>. If it is empty in the SERVICE bind
mode, an error is logged at startup, and the LDAP directory cannot be used
for logins (see the section on LDAP below).


! auth.ldap.bindMode

In the ACCOUNT mode, the account that searches and reads the entries, in any
letter case:

  * SERVICE: <*auth.ldap.bindUser*> searches the entry of the user, the user
    binds with its DN only to check the password, and <*auth.ldap.bindUser*>
    reads the entry and the group entries.
  * USER: the user binds with the name the user typed, and that connection
    searches and reads the entries.

If it is not set, the mode is SERVICE if <*auth.ldap.bindUser*> is set, and
USER otherwise. SERVICE without <*auth.ldap.bindUser*> is USER, and a warning
is logged at startup.


! auth.ldap.searchBase

In the DN mode (<*auth.ldap.authMode*> = DN, the default), the attributes of the
user are read from the user's entry, whose DN is built from <*auth.ldap.bindDN*>;
<*auth.ldap.searchBase*> is used instead only if <*auth.ldap.bindDN*> is not set,
with the same placeholder. In the ACCOUNT mode, it is the DN of the subtree in which
the user's entry is searched, without a placeholder. Defaults to <*auth.ldap.bindDN*>.


! auth.ldap.userObjectClass

The object class of the user entries (default "user", for Active Directory;
"inetOrgPerson" for OpenLDAP). Only entries of this class are found and read
as the entries of users.


! auth.ldap.groupObjectClass

The object class of the group entries that are read for
<*auth.ldap.rolePermissionAttribute*> (default "group", for Active Directory;
"groupOfNames" for OpenLDAP).


! auth.ldap.timeout

If user authentication via LDAP is enabled, this setting specifies the
timeout in seconds (default 30) for connecting to the LDAP server and for
each LDAP operation. An LDAP server that does not answer within this time
is treated as inaccessible. A value of 0 or less is replaced by the
default of 30 seconds, and a warning is logged at startup.


! auth.ldap.permissionAttribute

If user authentication via LDAP is enabled, this setting can be used to
configure the <[AuthAdminService]> to obtain user permissions from the LDAP
server. For that purpose, the LDAP server must provide permissions using
the LDAP attribute specified in this setting. If this setting is provided,
the user's permissions in the database are replaced with the permissions
that the LDAP server lists, also if it lists none, when a login that the
LDAP server has accepted is stored. A login that is accepted without that
store (from the password cache, or with the stored password, also after the
LDAP server has accepted the password and reading an entry has failed)
leaves them unchanged.


! auth.ldap.roleAttribute

If user authentication via LDAP is enabled, this setting can be used to
configure the <[AuthAdminService]> to obtain user role assignments from the
LDAP server. For that purpose, the LDAP server must provide role names
using the attribute specified in this setting. If this setting is
provided, the user's role assignments in the database are replaced with the
roles that are imported from the LDAP server (see <*auth.ldap.roleFilter*>
and <*auth.ldap.localRoles*>), also if there are none, when a login that the
LDAP server has accepted is stored. A login that is accepted without that
store leaves them unchanged (see <*auth.ldap.permissionAttribute*>). The
setting does not depend on <*auth.ldap.permissionAttribute*>.


! auth.ldap.rolePermissionAttribute

If user authentication via LDAP is enabled, this setting can be used to
obtain the permissions of the roles from the LDAP server. Each role
provided through <*auth.ldap.roleAttribute*> that is imported (see
<*auth.ldap.roleFilter*> and <*auth.ldap.localRoles*> in the section on LDAP
below) must then be the distinguished name of a group entry that the
connection reading the user's attributes can read (in the USER bind mode,
the user's own connection), and the permissions of the role are replaced
with the values of the attribute specified in this setting when the login is
stored (see <*auth.ldap.permissionAttribute*>). If such a group
entry cannot be read, the LDAP directory cannot be used for the login (see
the section on LDAP below). A role that is not imported needs no group
entry. If this setting is not provided, the imported roles are assigned and
their permissions are kept.


! auth.ldap.roleFilter

A regular expression for the LDAP roles that may be imported and assigned to
the user: a role is imported only if the expression matches its whole name,
which is in ASCII lower case, as described in the section on LDAP below:
<[ops|ops-.*]> does not match <[ops-read]>, <[(ops|ops-.*)$]> does. If it is
not set, every role may be imported. The local roles (the ones of
<*auth.ldap.localRoles*> and the roles created in the database while LDAP
authentication is enabled) are never imported (see the section on LDAP
below).


! auth.ldap.localRoles

A regular expression for the LDAP roles that are neither imported nor
assigned to the user, so that a local role with the same name keeps its
permissions (see the section on LDAP below). The role names are in ASCII
lower case, and the expression must match a whole name as described there:
<[admin|admins]> does not match <[admins]>, <[(admin|admins)$]> does.


! auth.ldap.localUsers

A regular expression for the names of local users, whose passwords are
checked against the database and not by the LDAP server. A user marked as an
LDAP user (user attribute <[auth_ldap]> = true) is an LDAP user even if the
expression matches its name, and a user marked as a local user
(<[auth_ldap]> = false) is a local user even if it does not. The expression
must match the whole name as described in the section on LDAP below:
<[admin|administrator]> does not match <[administrator]>,
<[(admin|administrator)$]> does. The name is the one given at the login if the
database has a user of that name, and otherwise that name with its ASCII
letters in lower case. With an invalid regular expression, the start of the
<[com.appinf.osp.auth.data]> bundle fails when it creates the
<[AuthAdminService]>.


! auth.ldap.extraAttributes

If user authentication via LDAP is enabled, this setting can be used to
configure the <[AuthAdminService]> to obtain additional user attributes from the
LDAP server containing application-specific information. The attribute
names must be separated by commas or semicolons. Once obtained from the LDAP server, the
attributes and their values can be queried using
the <[getUserAttribute()]> and <[attributesForUser()]> methods.

It is also possible to map an LDAP attribute name to a user attribute name,
by specifying both names separated by a colon. Example:

  auth.ldap.extraAttributes = givenname:firstName,surname:lastName
----

In the given example, the LDAP attribute <[givenname]> will be mapped to the
user attribute <[firstName]> and the LDAP attribute <[surname]> will be mapped
to <[lastName]>.

At most 8 attributes are read; if more are listed, a warning is logged at
startup, and only the first 8 are read. A user attribute name that starts with
<[auth_]>, in any letter case, is reserved for the <[AuthAdminService]>: the
value of an attribute mapped to such a name is not stored, and an error is
logged at startup.


! auth.ldap.inaccessible.fallbackToLocal

If true, a login of an LDAP user while the LDAP server is unavailable or the
LDAP directory cannot be used is checked against the copy of the password
stored in the database (see the section on LDAP below). Default is false.


! auth.ldap.cache.lifetimeDays

The number of days after the last successful LDAP login of a user for which
<*auth.ldap.inaccessible.fallbackToLocal*> allows the stored copy of the
password. Default is 7.


!! Using the AuthAdminService

The <[AuthAdminService]> provides methods for managing users, roles and permissions.
Please refer to the documentation of the Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthAdminService interface
and the Poco::OSP::Auth::Data::AuthAdminServiceImpl class for more information.

The <[AuthAdminService]> is registered under the service name "osp.auth" and
can be obtained using the Poco::OSP::ServiceFinder helper class:

    Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthAdminService::Ptr pAuthService =
        Poco::OSP::ServiceFinder::find<Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthAdminService>(pContext);
----


!! Managing Users, Roles and Permissions

In order to manage users, roles and permissions, the <*auth*> shell command is provided.
The command is implemented in the <*com.appinf.osp.auth.command*> bundle.

In order to use the <*auth*> command, the user must have the <*authAdmin*> permission.

From within the OSP Shell, enter <[auth]> command without arguments for a help screen.


! Creating a User Account

To create a user account, use the <*auth add user*> command and specify the
user name and its password as arguments. The following command will
create a user named alice with the password s3cr3t.

    osp> auth add user alice s3cr3t
----


! Granting a Permission to a User

To grant a permission to a user, use the <*auth grant user*> command, giving
the user name and permission as argument. For example, to grant user
alice access to the Bundle Administration Utility web application and related
shell commands:

    osp> auth grant user alice bundleAdmin
----


! Creating a Role

If multiple user accounts should have the same permissions, it makes
sense to define a role. This is done with the <*auth add role*> command. For
example, to define a role named <*superUsers*>:

    osp> auth add role superUsers
----


! Granting a Permission to a Role

To grant a permission to a role, use the <*auth grant role*> command, giving
the role name and permission as argument. For example, to grant role
<*superUsers*> access to the Bundle Administration Utility web application and related
shell commands:

    osp> auth grant role superUsers bundleAdmin
----


! Assigning a Role to a User

To assign a role to a user, use the <*auth assign*> command, giving the user
name and role name as arguments. For example, to assign user <*alice*> the
role <*superUsers*>:

    osp> auth assign alice superUsers
----


! Removing a Role From a User

To revoke a role from a user, use the <*auth unassign*> command, giving the
user name and role name as arguments. For example, to remove the role
<*superUsers*> from user <*alice*>:

    osp> auth unassign alice superUsers
----


! Revoking a Permission

A permission can be revoked from a user or role with the
<*auth revoke user*>, or <*auth revoke role*> command, respectively.
Specify the user or role name and permission as arguments. Example:

    osp> auth revoke user alice bundleAdmin
----


! Changing a User's Password

To change a user's password, use the <*auth passwd*> command, giving the
user name and new password as arguments. Example:

    osp> auth passwd alice sup3rs3cr3t
----


! Showing Information About Users, Roles and Permissions

To display information about a specific user, role or permission, or to
list all defined users, roles and permissions, use the <*auth show*>
command. For example, to show the <*admin*> user:

    osp> auth show user admin
----


!! Using LDAP for User Authentication and Authorization

Optionally, <[AuthAdminService]> can use LDAP for user authentication
and authorization. LDAP can be used for authentication (validating
passwords) only, or for both authentication and authorization (obtaining
a list of permissions and role assignments for an user).

To enable LDAP authentication, the following configuration settings must
be added to the global application configuration file:

  * auth.ldap.uri
  * auth.ldap.bindDN

To enable LDAP authorization, additionally one or both of the following
configuration settings must be added to the global application configuration file:

  * auth.ldap.permissionAttribute
  * auth.ldap.roleAttribute

Specifically, for LDAP authorization of permissions, the LDAP server must provide the
attribute specified in the <*auth.ldap.permissionAttribute*> configuration
setting. This attribute is used to store the permissions for a user.
In order to provide role assignments, the LDAP
server must provide the attribute specified in the
<*auth.ldap.roleAttribute*> configuration setting.

With these settings, the permissions that the LDAP server lists and the
roles imported from it replace the ones stored for that user in the database
when a login that the LDAP server has accepted is stored (see
<*auth.ldap.permissionAttribute*>).
Permissions and roles are obtained from the LDAP server immediately
after the user account has been successfully validated by performing an
LDAP bind operation with the user's credentials.

A login of an LDAP user with an empty password, or with a user name or
password that contains a NUL character, is rejected without contacting the
LDAP server, as the server may accept a bind without a password as an
unauthenticated bind.

The result of the bind with the user's credentials decides how a login
that the LDAP server does not accept fails:

  * invalidCredentials (49, also for a locked, disabled or expired account
    in Active Directory), inappropriateAuthentication (48),
    insufficientAccessRights (50), unwillingToPerform (53) and
    constraintViolation (19, for example from a password policy): the
    password is rejected. The login counts as a failed login, and the copy
    of the password that an LDAP login has stored in the database is cleared.
  * busy (51), unavailable (52), timeLimitExceeded (3), and a connection that
    fails, breaks or times out before the result arrives: the LDAP server is
    treated as unavailable (see below).
  * Any other result: the login fails and counts as a failed login, the
    stored copy of the password is kept, and a warning names the result code.

Failed logins are counted only for users that have an account in the
database. The wrong passwords of an LDAP user who has never logged in are
limited only by the LDAP server, for example by its password policy.

In the ACCOUNT mode (<*auth.ldap.authMode*> = ACCOUNT), a user can log in
with several names, such as <[alice]>, <[alice@company.com]> or
<[COMPANY\alice]>. The local account of the user, its failure count and
lock-out, the data stored from the LDAP server and the two-factor secret are
kept under the account name: the value of the attribute <[sAMAccountName]>
of the user's entry, with its ASCII letters in lower case. If the entry has
no <[sAMAccountName]>, and in the DN mode, the name the user typed, with its
ASCII letters in lower case, is used. An entry
with more than one <[sAMAccountName]> value is an error of the LDAP
directory. A login whose account name is the name of a local user, or matches
<*auth.ldap.localUsers*>, is refused.

In the USER bind mode, the LDAP server checks the password of the name the
user typed, and it may accept that name for another user than the entry found
by it, such as a user of another domain. So the entry is used only if the
typed name is one of its names, compared ignoring case: a name with a domain,
such as <[COMPANY\alice]>, must be the <[msDS-PrincipalName]> of the entry, a
name with <[@]> its <[userPrincipalName]>, and any other name its
<[sAMAccountName]>. Otherwise the login fails as for a bind result that
neither accepts nor rejects the password (see above), a warning is logged, and
nothing of the entry is stored. Logins as <[DOMAIN\name]> therefore need a
directory that provides <[msDS-PrincipalName]>, such as Active Directory; a
login with a <[userPrincipalName]> does not. The LDAP server binds a name
without a domain and without <[@]> in its own domain. If
<*auth.ldap.searchBase*> spans several domains that have an entry with that
<[sAMAccountName]>, the search finds several entries, and the LDAP directory
cannot be used for the login (see below).

In the SERVICE bind mode (<*auth.ldap.bindUser*> is set), the account name is
read before the password is sent to the LDAP server: a locked-out account is
refused through every name without a bind of the user, and a wrong password
counts under the account name. In the USER bind mode, the entry can be read
only after the LDAP server has accepted the password: a locked-out account is
refused through every name, but a wrong password counts under the name the
user typed, and only if a local account has that name; otherwise only the
lock-out of the LDAP server applies. The logins of a user wait for each other
while the LDAP server checks the password, so that concurrent logins get no
more guesses than the failure count allows: in the SERVICE bind mode through
every name of the user, in the USER bind mode only through the same name. Use
the SERVICE bind mode where the local lock-out must cover every name of a user.
While the LDAP server cannot be used, the stored copy of the password is
checked under the account name if the search of the SERVICE bind mode has read
it, otherwise under the name the user typed.

Applications log users in with Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthService::authenticateAccount()
and use the account name that it returns for the session and for the later
calls of the service. Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthService::authenticate() returns
false for a login with another name than the account name, and logs a warning.

Note: local accounts that earlier versions stored under another name of a
directory user, such as <[alice@company.com]>, are not used for the logins of
that user while the LDAP server can be used, as these use the account name.
While the LDAP server cannot be used and the account name has not been read,
a login with that name still logs in with the stored password of such an
account, without the lock-out and the two-factor secret of the account name.
Remove such accounts, and enable two-factor authentication for the user
again, under the account name.

Permission assignments to roles are always stored in the <[AuthAdminService]> database.

The names of the roles provided by the LDAP server are used with their ASCII
letters in lower case. The regular expressions of <*auth.ldap.roleFilter*>,
<*auth.ldap.localRoles*> and <*auth.ldap.localUsers*> are case-sensitive
except where they set the option i (a leading <[(?i)]> sets it for the whole
expression), and match a name only if the first non-empty match that the
expression finds at the start of the name covers the whole name. An
alternative that matches only the start of the name therefore hides a later
one that matches all of it: <[a|ab]> does not match <[ab]>. An expression
with alternatives puts them in a group followed by <[$]>, for example
<[(admin|admins)$]>; in <[admin|admins$]> the <[$]> belongs to the last
alternative only.

If <*auth.ldap.roleFilter*> is set, only the LDAP roles whose names match
its regular expression are imported and assigned to the user; the other
roles provided by the LDAP server are skipped. If it is not set, all roles
are imported, except the local roles below.

An LDAP role whose name matches the regular expression in
<*auth.ldap.localRoles*>, or equals the name of a role created in the
database while LDAP authentication is enabled, is neither imported nor
assigned to the user, so the local role keeps its permissions.

If the LDAP server is unavailable, or the LDAP directory cannot be used
(the service account is rejected, the search for the user's entry fails or
finds more than one entry, or the LDAP server accepts the password but the
user's entry or the permissions of one of the user's groups cannot be read),
the <[AuthAdminService]> checks the password against the copy stored in the
database only if <*auth.ldap.inaccessible.fallbackToLocal*> is true and the
last successful LDAP login of the user is not older than
<*auth.ldap.cache.lifetimeDays*> days. Otherwise the login fails. A password
that does not match the stored copy counts as a failed login, except after
the LDAP server has accepted it and only reading the user's entry or group
permissions has failed.


!!! Two-Factor Authentication

The <[com.appinf.osp.auth.twofactor]> bundle provides the
Poco::OSP::Auth::TwoFactor::TwoFactorService, which validates time-based
one-time passwords (TOTPs, RFC 6238) as a second factor. A code is
accepted for the current 30-second time step and for the steps before and
after it, and only once: after a code has been accepted, codes of the same
or an earlier time step are rejected for that user. The validations of one
instance of the service are serialised for this check, so instances that
share one SQL user database, such as those of several processes, can each accept
the same code once. Each call of the service uses the <[AuthAdminService]>
registered at that time, which keeps the secrets, the failure counts and the
lock-outs; without one, the call throws a Poco::NotFoundException.

Note: the second factor is checked only by a login handler of the application
that calls Poco::OSP::Auth::TwoFactor::TwoFactorService::validateTOTP(); no component
of OSP calls it. A mapping of the web server that must require the second factor
allows only session authentication (<[authMethods="session"]>), with the session
created by that login handler. HTTP Basic authentication and bearer tokens on other
mappings, the shell and the metrics server accept a user without the second factor.
Remote calls through the Auth/Remoting library take the user that the web server has
authenticated for the mapping of the call, so the rule for mappings applies to them.

The service is configured in the global application configuration file:

  * auth.twofactor.passphrase: the passphrase from which the key that
    encrypts the stored secrets is derived. Required.
  * auth.twofactor.issuer: the issuer name shown by authenticator apps. Required.
  * auth.twofactor.keySalt: the salt from which, with the passphrase, the key
    is derived. Optional; see below.
  * auth.twofactor.secretLengthBits: the length of a generated secret in bits
    (default 128), a multiple of 8 and at least 80.
  * auth.twofactor.codeDigits: the number of digits of a code (default 6),
    from 6 to 8.
  * auth.twofactor.maxFailures: the number of wrong codes since the last
    accepted code at which the user is locked out (default 5). A value of 0
    disables the count and the lock-out.
  * auth.twofactor.lockOutPeriod: the time in seconds for which the user
    is locked out (default 600). While the user is locked out, every code
    is rejected and the <[AuthAdminService]> rejects the password.

The bundle does not start if <*auth.twofactor.secretLengthBits*> or
<*auth.twofactor.codeDigits*> is out of range. Without <*auth.twofactor.passphrase*>
or <*auth.twofactor.issuer*>, the bundle starts, logs a notice and registers no
service. The bundle requires the module <[osp.auth]> 1.8.0 or newer, which the
<[com.appinf.osp.auth.data]> bundle provides.

A wrong code is counted only while the user is not locked out; a valid code
of the time step of the last accepted code, or of an earlier one, is rejected
but not counted. The count is kept until a code is accepted or two-factor
authentication is disabled for the user, so after a lock-out has ended, the
next wrong code locks the user out again. The lock-out is set in the
<[AuthAdminService]>, as after failed logins, and applies to every user, also
to the users of <*auth.neverLockOutUsers*>. A login handler that checks the
code only after the password has been accepted keeps others from locking such
a user out with wrong codes. The <*auth unlock*> shell
command (Poco::OSP::Auth::AuthAdminService::unlockOutUser()) ends the lock-out
and resets the count of failed logins, but not the count of wrong codes: after
an unlock of this lock-out, the next wrong code locks the user out again, until
a code is accepted or two-factor authentication is disabled for the user.

The secrets are stored as user attributes, encrypted with AES-256-GCM and
bound to the user name. The key is derived from <*auth.twofactor.passphrase*>
and <*auth.twofactor.keySalt*>. If <*auth.twofactor.keySalt*> is not set,
the bundle generates a random salt at its first start and keeps it in its
preferences (setting <*keySalt*>). If the passphrase or the salt changes, or
the preferences of the bundle with the generated salt are lost, the stored
secrets cannot be decrypted, and two-factor authentication must be enabled
again for the users.

A secret encrypted with AES-256-CBC and a key derived from the passphrase and a
fixed salt is read as well, and is stored again with AES-256-GCM after the first
accepted code; software that reads only AES-256-CBC secrets cannot read it then.

Several instances that share one SQL user database must use the same
<*auth.twofactor.passphrase*> and the same <*auth.twofactor.keySalt*>, so
that each instance can decrypt the secrets that another one has stored.
